### Held back, on purpose

- TypeScript stays at 6.0.3: TypeScript 7 is out, but typescript-eslint and dependency-cruiser do not support it yet.
- pnpm stays at 11: pnpm 12 is too new to trust with this project's supply-chain settings.

### Not using, on purpose

The install hook (`.claude/hooks/verify-install.sh`) denies these packages and dependency-cruiser forbids importing
them. Changing that is a human decision: write a new ADR, then install by hand.

| Not using | Why | Use instead |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Next.js App Router / RSC | Repeated critical security advisories | TanStack Start |
| Prisma | An unpinned install now pulls a release candidate that breaks projects | Kysely |
| Drizzle | Waiting for 1.0 | Kysely |
| Redis/Valkey, Meilisearch | Postgres covers caching, queues and search for now | Postgres, pg-boss, full-text search |
| Storybook | Only needed for a documented design system | Vitest browser mode |
| Turborepo/Nx | Only needed in a monorepo | just |
| Inngest/Trigger.dev | pg-boss covers jobs and cron | pg-boss |
| Unleash | Now AGPL | OpenFeature + flagd |
| Clerk | Passkeys are not on its free plan | better-auth |

### Patterns not used, on purpose

Generic architecture prompts often ask for these. Each one weakens a gate this project relies on.

| Pattern | Why not | Use instead |
| --- | --- | --- |
| An in-process event bus or pub/sub between features | dependency-cruiser, the types and grep cannot see who handles an event, and an event in memory is lost on a crash | Direct calls through a module's `index.ts` (ADR 0003); pg-boss jobs for side effects that must survive a restart |
| Fallback defaults for missing required configuration | A missing secret or database URL must stop the boot; a default auth secret in production lets anyone sign sessions | `src/env.ts` validates at boot: secrets and the database URL are required in production, and only safe values (port, log level) have defaults |
| An adapter around every SDK | A wrapper with a single implementation adds indirection and hides the SDK's own types; providers are rarely swapped | One port per capability the app needs, declared where the app uses it (`src/app/ports.ts`, or next to the route that needs it) and implemented in `src/infra`; tests pass small real fakes |
| One `/config` folder for every constant | Every feature then depends on one hub, and deployment settings get mixed with code constants | `src/env.ts` for what differs per deployment, `flags/flags.json` for runtime switches{% if kind == 'web' %}, the Tailwind `@theme` tokens for design{% endif %}, constants next to the code that owns them |

### Reviewed exceptions

- Licence: `sentry` (the Sentry CLI that `@sentry/node` 11 ships for source-map upload) is FSL-1.1-Apache-2.0, which
  becomes Apache-2.0 two years after each release. It is the only package allowed outside the SPDX allowlist
  (`justfile` `licenses`, `supply-chain.yml`).
- Supply-chain policy: `semver`'s legacy 5.x/6.x releases are excluded from `trustPolicy: no-downgrade` by exact
  version (published after 7.x gained provenance); a security release younger than the 3-day `minimumReleaseAge` is
  excluded by exact version with a removal date (`pnpm-workspace.yaml`).

## Consequences

- One process and one image to run; the database is the only stateful dependency.
- New capabilities start from this list. When something here is replaced, this ADR is superseded by a new one in the
  same change.
